(5) your plan on carrying out each method that your team chose by answering the following questions:


Option (A): Interviews
What are 5 assumptions that you are making about the direct/indirect stakeholders and 5 things you might need/want to know about them / their work process. Based on this brainstorming, come up with 3 high-level questions. For each high-level question, come up with at least 5 specific interview questions. In total, you should have 3 high-level questions, and 15 specific questions. Explain how you avoided "bad" questions as explained in the lecture. Finally, describe your plan for recruiting interviewees, obtaining their consent, how many people you plan to interview, and when/where these interviews will be conducted.


5 Assumptions About Stakeholders 
1. Healthcare providers do not have enough time to effectively[d] prepare each child for surgical procedures 
2. Children who understand their surgical process and treatment will experience less fear and anxiety 
3. Parents play a large role in preparing and reassuring children before surgery 
4. Different children require different levels of preparation based on age, prior experience and maturity. 
5. Existing resources do not adequately prepare children for every type of procedure and are not accessible to all families. 


5 Things We Want to Learn
1. How healthcare providers currently prepare children for surgery/treatment 
2. What parts of the experience are most involved in increasing anxiety for children 
3. How effective are the current processes used to inform families of their child’s treatment plan [e]
4. What barriers prevent healthcare providers from providing a more extensive preparation or education 
5. What features could realistically support children, parents and healthcare providers without creating additional workload requiring more resources. 


3 High Level Questions 
1. How are families currently prepared for their child’s treatment process? 
* Can you walk us through what typically happens from when a child is diagnosed until the day of their procedure? 
* On the day of their procedure, what reinforcements[f] are put into place to continue to help children prepare for the process? 
* What resources are provided to families post-procedure to continue ongoing care for their child? [g]
* Which healthcare professionals are typically responsible for preparing children and families for the procedure? 
* What factors influence how much preparation/what kind of preparation a child receives for surgery? [h]
2. What challenges do children, families and healthcare providers experience during the pre and post operative process? 
* What parts of the surgical process tend to be the most challenging for children? 
* Can you describe a situation where a child seemed particularly unprepared for their procedure and what the consequences of that were? [i]
* What challenges do parents commonly experience when preparing their child for surgery? [j]
* After surgery, what questions or concerns do children and families most commonly have? 
* What prevents healthcare providers from spending more time preparing children for procedures? 
3. What would make pediatric surgical preparation more effective? 
* What types of resources have you seen work well in preparing children for medical procedures? 
* What types of resources have not worked well in preparing children for medical procedures? [k]
* How do preparation needs differ for children with different levels of maturity? 
* What information do you think is key for children to understand prior to their procedure? 
* How would you quantify the effectiveness of a preparation tool for families? 


How We Avoided Bad Questions
We designed our interview questions to avoid leading, closed-ended, hypothetical or broad questions. As opposed to asking participants whether they believe a solution would be useful, we focused on learning about the current processes, experiences and consequences of the system. We specifically ask participants to describe how children are currently prepared, what strategies have been working and which ones have not been effective. 


We avoid questions that assume our initial assumptions which allows participants to identify the constraints, limitations, and barriers they experience that we may not have considered. Most questions are open-ended and encourage participants to discuss real experiences. 


Recruiting Interviewees 
We plan to recruit approximately 5 interview participants representing multiple stakeholder perspectives. Ideally this would include pediatric nurses, doctors and any other healthcare professionals involved in surgical care. Additionally we will recruit families who have previously supported a child through a surgical procedure. [l]Healthcare professionals and families will be recruited within our team's network. 


Before each interview, participants will be informed of the explicit purpose of this project, the expected length of the interview and that participation is entirely voluntary. We will obtain informed consent before beginning the interview. Participants will also be told they may decline to answer any question or exit the interview at any given point. All identifying information will be excluded from our notes and final project materials unless provided explicit permission otherwise. 


Interviews will take approximately 15 minutes[m] and will be conducted virtually over the phone. Interviews will be completed during the contextual inquiry stage of the project so that findings may inform the development of our solution. 


Option (C): Literature Research
Describe your target problem and identify keywords you will use to search for literature on this topic. When brainstorming keywords, consider who is affected by the problem, what barriers exist (cost, time, access, trust, policy, etc.), and when, where, or how the problem occurs. Starting with a simple search (e.g. [keyword 1] AND [keyword 2]), search for literature in two of the recommended library databases (Pitchbook, Scopus, and/or Statista). Describe 2 ways that you would improve your search strategy for a future search.




Our target problem is the lack of consistent and effective preparation for children and their families during medical treatment. Children may experience fear, anxiety and uncertainty while parents may feel unprepared to support their child. Healthcare providers could help reduce this uncertainty, but time and resources constraints seem to limit the effectiveness of the current individualized support systems in place. Our literature research will focus on understanding how pediatric patients are currently prepared for surgery, the barriers healthcare providers face when providing support and what systems could be implemented to improve children and family experiences. 


Potential keywords include: pediatric surgery, pediatric patients, children, preoperative preparation, postoperative care, surgical anxiety, preoperative anxiety, patient education, family education, parental anxiety, child-life interventions, procedural preparation, healthcare communication, time constraints, healthcare resources, digital health tools, educational interventions, surgical experience, and patient engagement.


After conducting our initial search, there are two main ways we could improve our search strategy. First, refine and expand our keywords based on terminology found in relevant articles. For example including both pediatric surgery OR pediatric surgical care. This would hel;p us identify literature that discusses the same concepts using different terminology. 


Secondly, we could apply more specific search filters. For example, we could limit the results to peer-reviewed articles published within the last 10 years with pediatric populations and hospital settings. We could narrow the search further by age group or type of intervention to further identify more directly relevant articles. This would make the literature more targeted to the users of our design. 










