Lööf, G., & Lönnqvist, P. A. (2022). Role of information and preparation for improvement of pediatric perioperative care. Paediatric anaesthesia, 32(5), 600–608. https://doi.org/10.1111/pan.14419


* Large amounts of evidence indicating that preparation can improve anxiety, cooperation and recovery during pre-, intra-, and post-operative procedures 
* Many children say they feel underprepared for pre-, intra- and post-operative procedures 
* Individual temperament and parent’s anxiety increase risk for anxiety during pre-, intra-, and post-op procedures
* Current methods of preparation should be re-evaluated 
   * Methods of preparation should move away from only providing information and focus on helping children understand the information 
* Recommended Interventions:
   * Recommends interactive, age-specific, web based technology to help children prepare for pre-, intra- and post-operative procedures 
   * Pharmacological interventions:
      * Pharmacological interventions were relied on in the past 
      * Reduced stress, controlled reflexes, counteracted side effects of anesthesia
      * The development of distraction and preparation interventions have reduced the need for pharmacological interventions
      * Non-pharmacological interventions have become increasingly important
         * Reduce negative side effects
         * Helps children learn to cope with situation 
         * Non pharmacological interventions are equally effective as pharmacological solutions in reducing anxiety 
   * Social Interventions:
      * Parents should have the option to be with children when receiving anesthesia
         * Anesthesia is a stressful situation for the child > parent presence could be helpful 
         * Presence of caregiver could provide psychological support 
            * Helps child adapt to situation
         * Parents can provide insight into children’s coping strategies and responses
         * Parents can help children regulate their stress 
   * Behavioural interventions
      * Behaviour of healthcare providers can impact children’s anxiety 
         * distracting/unrelated/humourous discussions reduce children’s anxiety 
         * Emotional responses (like reassurance and empathy) is associated with increased stress
   * Environmental interventions
      * Anesthesia and entering operating room are most stressful part of medical procedures for children 
      * Transporting children to operating room in toy car linked to reduced anxiety 
      * Providing anesthesia in a different room (separate from the operating room) 
   * Psychological interventions
      * Distraction effective in reducing anxiety 
         * Focused on relaxing or entertaining material rather than the procedure 
      * Distractions include: clowns, video games, apps, videos, virtual reality, hypnosis, guided imagery
      * Difference between distraction and education materials
         * Distraction reduces stress by distracting children from what they are afraid of 
         * Educational materials reduces stress by helping children understand the procedure and be more prepared 
   * Educational interventions
      * educational preparation initiatives reduce stress, improve recovery and coping 
      * Educational materials reduce anxiety compared to distracting materials 
      * Most effective way to help children learn to cope in similar situations 
   * Educational preparation
      * How children view the procedure will impact how they act in response to the procedure 
      * Providing accurate information helps children will reduce fears
      * Preparation helps build trust in the medical system 
      * Preparation helps children know what to expect regarding the procedure 
      * Preparation should include the following:
         * Providing information
         * Help children cope 
         * Modelling
            * Behaviours can be learned by watching others
            * Teach children how to act 
            * Most effective when the child identifies with the model (e.g. are the same age)
      * Important to consider how the children understand the information 
         * Children’s ability to learn and understand information differs depending on age
            * Information given to patients needs to take this into consideration 
      * Stress can also impact children's ability to understand the information
      * Information provided should discuss the procedure and what they child will feel 
      * Children emphasize importance of receiving information in different formats 
         * Important to be exposed to the information repeatedly 
      * Helpful to receive information before the procedure 
         * Gives children time to understand the information 
   * Current preparation methods include:
      * Books and booklets
         * Effectiveness is unclear 
      * Therapeutic play/theatre 
         * Effectiveness is also unclear
   * Multicomponent prep
      * reduces anxiety in children and parents 
      * No evidence has been found on if/how if affects patient adherence to post-op instructions 
   * Audiovisual and interactive methods:
      * Reduces anxiety and increased adherence during pre-, intra- and post-op procedures 
      * Results are most consistent when using handheld devices (but all technology is shown to reduce anxiety) 
      * Games provide information to the patient in a way that is less scary 
         * Games must prepare children for the procedure 
      * Distraction games also help reduce anxiety 
      * Education videos reduce parental anxiety and increase parental knowledge about the procedures


Lööf, G., Liljeberg, C., Eksborg, S., & Lönnqvist, P.-A. (2017). Interactive web-based format vs conventional brochure material for information transfer to children and parents: A randomized controlled trial regarding preoperative information. Paediatric Anaesthesia, 27(6), 657–664. https://doi.org/10.1111/pan.13142


* Compared an interactive preparation website with brochures before outpatient surgery
   * Children were aged 3–12; 125 were recruited and 103 were included in the final analysis
   * Children and parents answered six knowledge questions on the day of surgery
* Children using the website had higher knowledge scores
   * Median scores were 27/36 for the website and 19.5/36 for brochures
   * Older children scored higher in both groups, so the same material may not suit every age
* Relevance to KareBEAR
   * Include a few questions to check what children understand after each story
   * Measure understanding separately from enjoyment or anxiety
* The study did not test AI, long-term recall, or preparation during emergency surgery


Fortier, M. A., Bunzli, E., Walthall, J., Olshansky, E., Saadat, H., Santistevan, R., Mayes, L., & Kain, Z. N. (2015). Web-based tailored intervention for preparation of parents and children for outpatient surgery (WebTIPS): Formative evaluation and randomized controlled trial. Anesthesia & Analgesia, 120(4), 915–922. https://doi.org/10.1213/ANE.0000000000000632


* WebTIPS provided separate preparation websites for children and parents
   * Combined animations, information about surgery, coping strategies, and interactive games
   * The randomized phase enrolled 82 children aged 2–7 undergoing elective outpatient surgery
* Children receiving WebTIPS showed less anxiety than those receiving standard care
   * Differences were found at operating-room entry and introduction of the anesthesia mask
   * Parents also reported less anxiety in the preoperative holding area
* Relevance to KareBEAR
   * Give caregivers guidance alongside the child’s story so they can help explain unfamiliar steps
   * Include what children may see and feel, plus simple ways to cope
* The study tested a package of activities, so it does not show which feature caused the improvement
   * It also does not show that AI would improve the results


Carroll, A. R., Johnson, J. A., Stassun, J. C., Greevy, R. A., Mixon, A. S., & Williams, D. J. (2024). Health literacy–informed communication to reduce discharge medication errors in hospitalized children: A randomized clinical trial. JAMA Network Open, 7(1), e2350969. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2023.50969


* Studied 198 caregivers of children aged six or younger who were prescribed a new liquid medication
   * Compared standard counseling with picture-based instructions, teach-back, a dosing demonstration, and show-back
      * Teach-back asks the caregiver to explain an instruction in their own words
      * Show-back asks the caregiver to demonstrate how they would carry it out
* Among 151 caregivers with follow-up dosing data, incorrect doses occurred in 30.4% of the intervention group versus 54.2% of the standard-counseling group
* Relevance to KareBEAR
   * Giving information is not enough; we should check whether families can use it
   * A caregiver-assisted question such as “Can you tell me what happens next?” could help reveal misunderstandings
* The findings concern caregiver medication dosing, not children’s use of an app
   * The study cannot separate teach-back from the other activities or validate automated AI feedback


Li, H. C. W., Lopez, V., & Lee, T. L. I. (2007). Effects of preoperative therapeutic play on outcomes of school-age children undergoing day surgery. Research in Nursing & Health, 30(3), 320–332. https://doi.org/10.1002/nur.20191


* Compared therapeutic play with routine information before day surgery
   * 203 school-age children were invited to participate in the randomized trial
* Children receiving therapeutic play reported lower anxiety before and after surgery
   * They also showed fewer negative emotions when anesthesia was introduced
   * Postoperative pain did not differ significantly between the groups
* Relevance to our non-tech solution
   * Explore guided play and equipment demonstrations in the proposed community preparation sessions
   * This would give children a chance to become familiar with the procedure and ask questions
* The study was conducted around hospital day surgery, so it does not establish that community sessions would have the same effects




