(2) Describe how you will integrate this feedback into your prototype development (1 paragraph, 100 words max)


We will include various recommendations into our prototype. We will use AI to simplify medical information provided to parents to make it easier to understand. We will allow users to choose their age range and use AI to tailor the information and the display depending on age and cognitive abilities. Additionally, our app will include ways to distract children from stressful medical procedures (like getting needles). Distractions can include jokes, colouring, or games.In our non-tech solution we will give children and families a paper booklet that includes basic information about surgeries and preparation methods. There will also be community learning groups as part of our non-tech solution, which will be run by volunteers. 


(3) Considering your tech (i.e., AI) and non-tech solution -- are they each a standalone solution that can by itself adequately address the target problem? 
    ● If so, provide your reasoning. Which solution (tech vs non-tech) do you feel to be stronger and why?
    ● If the solutions are not standalone solutions, provide your reasoning. Can these solutions be combined to address different aspects of the target problem? 


If we want to address a wider population of children and improve post-operative care across the board, a combination of both our AI and non-technical solutions would be ideal. One of the community partners we spoke with brought up a great point that not every family is comfortable giving their child access to technology and might opt for the non-tech solution over a catered AI one. This gives families both virtual and physical spaces where they can build trust and comfortability within the healthcare space for their kids.


(4) As a team, decide and describe the revised/refined tech and non-tech solution you plan to move forward with. 


Revised AI solution
We will be creating an app that aims to provide educational material and post-op instructions to kids, parents, and adults with intellectual disabilities, in an easily understandable manner. The pre-op portion of the app will contain basic knowledge about common surgical experiences (administration of the BP cuff, taking vitals, getting anesthesia, listening through the stethoscope and wearing a hospital gown), as well as a very basic summary of their specific procedure, with the option to learn more. There will also be ways to distract the kids during stressful situations (e.g. getting a needle) built into the app.


The post-op portion of the app will contain medicine adherence and surgery specific instructions that the child can follow after surgery. These instructions would be integrated through an AI transcription feature within the app, which presents the added benefit of having a concrete place where the information can live, in case a parent forgets. The bear would be “experiencing” these events alongside the child, and encouraging them to adhere. The child would receive “points” for adhering as well and would be able to use these points to purchase fun things within the app. This gamification will encourage adherence. Finally, there would be an information section containing links to local support options and care information for parents.


In general, the AI will allow the app to be compatible with different languages and easily understandable for lay audiences. Furthermore, parents will have the ability to see and release all of the information to the kids, before they get to see it. This will ensure that all of the information is accurate, and that parents feel a part of the care process as well.


Revised Non-AI solution


We will aim to launch community engagement sessions to educate kids about what to expect at the hospital when undergoing surgical procedures. There will be demos of common equipment and games to prepare them and form positive associations with the hospital environment. Kids/families will leave with a KareBEAR book in their preferred language (i.e. Pennsylvania Dutch for Mennonite communities) that will include stickers and games to help adhere to the post-op regimen, as well as a section to write down post-op instructions from providers. 


The sessions will be run by volunteers and/or medical students, to reduce the burden of providing the education from direct providers (i.e. surgeons, nurses), and make the solution cost-effective. 


