[a]super interesting problem! I recommend finding a focus point here and specify the part of the 'experience' you want to improve. It is already really specific, but think about where the experience is suboptimal and pick one of those areas that technology could address (remembering that many issues tend to be structural, which technology cannot address).
[b]interviews are a bit more work in terms of coming up with questions/note taking and then analyzing findings, so I would instead recommend 4 people for interviews and 2 people for literature review.
[c]I would also recommend trying to use observation as an inquiry method. Something like just sitting in the ER and observing the amount of children coming in, etc.
[d]'effectively' seems really broad... what does that mean? Do you have any examples of what that looks like?
[e]again, I'd think more about how you would measure effectiveness.
[f]I'd use a different (more accessible) word, this seems hard to understand
[g]I would ask questions that focus on their first-hand experience rather than third-person questions. So instead, ask 'what resources do you provide to families post-procedure?'. This helps prevent people from going off on tangents about things they know about but don't experience/do themselves
[h]make sure that you are asking questions that are asking for the interview participant's own experiences/knowledge. This sort of question is better answered via literature review.
[i]good question!
